WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.590
For suitable shale-rich parent material, increasing metamorphic grade can produce slate,

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.850
then phyllite, schist, and gneiss.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:12.310
Slate is dull and fine grained, phyllite has a sheen, schist

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.990
has visible aligned minerals, and gneiss commonly displays compositional bands.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:22.930
Quartz-rich sandstone can become quartzite as quartz grains recrystallize and interlock;

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.850
fractures may cut across grains.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.990
Limestone can become marble, a rock of recrystallized carbonate minerals.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.810
Both can have weak foliation, so use composition and texture together.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.570
Mafic volcanic rocks can produce greenstone or other metamorphic rocks as

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:42.450
mineral assemblages change. Felsic volcanic and plutonic rocks have different compositions

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.990
and can form different products.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.870
A metamorphic name depends on the rock and its observable features,

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:48.790
not grade alone.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.930
Hornfels commonly forms when rock is heated near an intrusion.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.030
Gneissic banding can form in several parent compositions, and the parent

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.110
of a particular gneiss may remain uncertain.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:04.450
Mineralogy, grain size, fabric, and field context jointly support identification.
